## What I built — the structured chain prover (the last piece of open item 1)

The S495 verifier was already Õ(√x), so the binding cost was the delegated chain **prover**: `inner_verify_div`'s backward sweep materialized five `2^{2ℓ}≈p²`-size tables per chain layer → **O(n·p²)/Lucy-layer → Σ_{p≤√x} n·p² ≈ Õ(x^{3/2})**, reintroducing the very exponent the compressed DP removed.

`chain_layer_reduce_structured` (in `lucy_dp_delegated_wiring.py`, opt-in `structured=True`) removes it. The summand `F(σ′,σ)=E(σ′)P(σ′)R_j(σ′,σ)L(σ)v_j(σ)` **factors**: E,P depend only on σ′; L,v only on σ; R_j is the **sparse width-4 affine operator** (4 nonzeros/column). Splitting the 2ℓ-variable sum-check at the σ′/σ boundary runs both phases over **O(p)-size** tables — phase 1 over `E·P·G` with `G(y)=Σ_σ R_j(y,σ)L(σ)v_j(σ)` built in O(p); phase 2 over `(κR*)·L·v`. **Per chain layer O(p²)→O(p); whole chain Σ_p n·p ≈ Õ(x).**

## Why it's a safe drop-in (verified, not asserted)

The transcript is **bit-identical**: sum-check challenges come from `rng`, never from the eval values, and the round polynomials are the unique multilinear sum-check of the *same* F — G merely regroups the σ-sum, which commutes with multilinear extension. The verifier checks are untouched, and `accept_rem` (affine-image path) is orthogonal.

Selftest cases 5/6/6b assert **per-round-polynomial equality exhaustively** (small p, random points/chain-vectors) plus equal accept/reject + comm on truth / truth+1 / lying-chain / corrupt-DP / false-wiring, across both the division and affine-image paths. Downstream (`compressed_layer --selftest`, base) pass **verbatim** (structured is opt-in, default dense). End-to-end at x=2¹⁶−1, structured and dense are identical (π=6542, comm=77133, both liars rejected).

